.TH PYSH 1 "" "pysh 0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pysh \- a shell with an embedded Python interpreter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pysh
.RB [ \-e ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR command " [" arg " ...]]"
.br
.B pysh
.RI [ script " [" arg " ...]]"
.br
.B pysh
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help | \-V | \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pysh
is a single, binary (statically linked when built with `make static`) that is both a command shell and a Python
read\-eval\-print loop.
Each line is run as Python if it parses as Python and does not begin with a name
that is not bound in Python but is an executable, builtin or alias;
otherwise it is run as a shell command.
So
.B x = 6*7
and
.B x
are Python, while
.B ls \-la
and
.B git status
are shell commands.
.PP
Python is the CPython the binary was built against.
Its standard library and site\-packages are located through the same prefix as the
system
.BR python ,
so the same pure\-Python modules are importable.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " command"
Run
.I command
(shell and Python lines, separated by newlines) and exit.
Remaining arguments become
.IR sys.argv .
.TP
.B \-e
Stop at the first command that fails (scripts and
.BR \-c ).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and a summary of the syntax and exit.
The same text is printed by the
.B help
builtin.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the pysh and Python versions and the Python home, then exit.
.TP
.I script
Read and run lines from a file.
.PP
With no arguments pysh is interactive when standard input and output are terminals.
Otherwise it reads commands from standard input.
.SH SCRIPTS
A script is a file of shell and Python lines, run in order.
Start it with a shebang line and make it executable to run it like any program:
.PP
.RS
.nf
#!/usr/bin/pysh
import sys
for host in sys.argv[1:]:
    r = !(ping \-c1 \-W1 @(host))
    print(host, "up" if r else "down")
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B #!/usr/bin/env pysh
works too, and one option may follow the interpreter
.RB ( "#!/usr/bin/pysh \-e" ).
.IP \(bu 3
The script path is
.IR sys.argv[0] ,
the arguments follow, and
.I __name__
is
.BR __main__ .
.IP \(bu 3
A Python block ends at the first line that is not indented, so shell lines can follow it directly.
.IP \(bu 3
An uncaught Python exception prints a traceback with the real file name and line number and ends
the script with status 1.
A failing shell command does not end it (its status is in
.IR last.returncode ),
unless
.B \-e
is given.
A name that is not a command reports
.B command not found
(status 127) with the script name and line.
.IP \(bu 3
The exit status is that of the last command, or the argument of
.BR exit .
.IP \(bu 3
.B $name
in a shell line is an environment variable; use
.BI @( name )
for a Python variable.
.SH PYTHON AND SHELL TOGETHER
.TP
.BI $( cmd )
Run
.I cmd
and evaluate to its standard output (trailing newline removed) as a string.
Inside a shell command it is substituted into the word, and, when unquoted,
split on whitespace.
.TP
.BI !( cmd )
Run
.I cmd
and evaluate to a
.B Result
object with the attributes
.BR returncode ,
.BR out ,
.BR err ,
.B duration
and
.BR cmd .
A Result is true if the return code is zero.
.TP
.BI @( expr )
In a shell command, evaluate the Python expression
.IR expr .
A list or tuple becomes several words.
.TP
.BI $ NAME
In Python, an item of the environment:
.B $HOME
and
.BR "$EDITOR = 'vi'" .
In a shell command, ordinary variable expansion, including
.B $?
for the last return code.
.TP
.BI ![ cmd ]
Start
.I cmd
and return a
.B Process
at once, to be read while it runs.
See
.BR STREAMING .
.TP
.BI $[ cmd ]
Run
.I cmd
attached to the terminal (live output, interactive programs work) and return its
.BR Result .
.TP
.BR sh( cmd ", ...)"
Run a command from Python with options
.RB ( capture ", " tee ", " check ", " timeout ", " stream ", " merge ).
See
.BR STREAMING .
.TP
.B % cmd
A line that starts with
.B %
is always a shell command.
See
.BR "FORCING SHELL MODE" .
.TP
.B last
The
.B Result
of the most recent command.
.TP
.B aliases
A dictionary of aliases.
A string value replaces the first word of a command; a callable is called with the
argument list and may return a string to print or an integer exit status.
.SH FORCING SHELL MODE
pysh decides per line whether it is Python or a shell command.
A line that begins with
.B %
(after optional blanks) is always a shell command, whatever it looks like:
.PP
.RS
.nf
% export X=5; sh \-c 'echo $X'
% echo 1 + 2
.fi
.RE
.PP
The first line sets an environment variable and runs a command in one line, which would otherwise be
parsed as Python; the second prints
.B 1 + 2
instead of evaluating it.
The
.B %
is removed before the line is run.
It is only special at the start of a line, so
.B x % 4
is still modulo.
Highlighting and Tab completion treat the rest of the line as a shell command.
.SH STREAMING
.BR $(cmd) " and " !(cmd)
wait for the command to finish.
.BI ![ cmd ]
instead returns a
.B Process
immediately, so output can be read while the command is still running:
.PP
.RS
.nf
for line in ![tail \-f /var/log/syslog]:
    if "error" in line: print(line)

with ![make \-j8] as p:
    for line in p:
        print(line)
print(p.returncode)
.fi
.RE
.PP
Only a single pipeline can be streamed; lists with
.BR ; ,
.B &&
or
.B &
are rejected.
Standard error stays on the terminal unless
.B merge=True
is given to
.BR sh() .
.SS Process
.TP
.RB "iteration, " readline() ", " read()
Lines without the trailing newline, one line (including its newline), or everything left.
Iteration ends when the command's output ends, and then waits for it to exit.
.TP
.BI wait( timeout )
Wait for the command to exit and return its status (128+N if killed by signal N).
Raises
.B subprocess.TimeoutExpired
if
.I timeout
passes first.
.TP
.BR poll() ", " returncode
The status if the command has exited, otherwise
.BR None .
.TP
.BR terminate() ", " kill() ", " send_signal( sig )
Signal every process in the pipeline.
.TP
.B result
A
.B Result
(without captured output) once the command has exited.
.TP
.BR pid ", " timed_out ", " cmd
.TP
.B with ![cmd] as p:
Ends the command (SIGTERM, then SIGKILL after three seconds) if the block is left before it has exited.
.SS $[cmd]
Runs
.I cmd
in the foreground attached to the terminal: output appears immediately, interactive programs such as
editors work, Ctrl\-Z stops them, and a
.B Result
is returned (its
.B out
and
.B err
are
.BR None ).
.SS sh()
.B sh(cmd, capture=True, tee=False, check=False, timeout=None, stream=False, merge=False)
.TP
.B capture
Collect standard output and error in the Result (default).
.B capture=False
is the same as
.BR $[cmd] .
.TP
.B tee
Collect them
.I and
show them live.
.TP
.B check
Raise
.B ShellError
if the status is not zero.
The exception has
.B returncode
and
.B result
attributes.
.TP
.B timeout
Stop the command after this many seconds: SIGTERM to its whole process group, then SIGKILL two seconds
later, and raise
.BR subprocess.TimeoutExpired .
Because the whole group is signalled, a command that has started children cannot keep the call waiting.
.TP
.B stream
Return a
.B Process
(same as
.BR ![cmd] ).
.TP
.B merge
With
.BR stream ,
send standard error into the stream.
.PP
Inside a function,
.BI @( expr )
sees the function's local variables, and these calls work from threads.
.SH WORKING DIRECTORY
.B $PWD
always names the real working directory.
If the process that started pysh left an unset, relative or stale
.B PWD
in the environment (for example a Windows path when started through
.BR wsl.exe ),
pysh replaces it at startup, and child processes see the corrected value.
.B cd
keeps symbolic links logical, as bash does:
.B cd link; cd ..
goes to the directory containing the link.
.B cd \-P
resolves links,
.B cd \-
returns to
.BR $OLDPWD ,
and the
.B pwd
builtin prints the logical path
.RB ( "pwd \-P"
the physical one).
.SH SHELL SYNTAX
Pipelines (\fB|\fR, and \fB|&\fR which also pipes standard error),
lists (\fB;\fR, \fB&&\fR, \fB||\fR), background jobs (\fB&\fR),
redirections (\fB>\fR, \fB>>\fR, \fB<\fR, \fB2>\fR, \fB2>&1\fR, \fB>&2\fR, \fB&>\fR),
single and double quotes, backslash escapes, globbing,
tilde expansion, and
.IB NAME = value
prefixes on a command are supported.
There are no shell functions, loops or conditionals: use Python.
.SS Brace expansion
.B {a,b,c}
expands to separate words, and
.BR {1..5} ,
.BR {10..1..3} ,
.B {01..10}
and
.B {a..e}
to sequences.
Braces may be nested and combined with a prefix and suffix:
.BR "mkdir \-p src/{lib,bin}/{a,b}" .
Quoted braces and braces without a comma or range are left alone.
.SS Arithmetic
.BI $(( expression ))
is replaced by the value of an integer expression with
.BR "+ \- * / % **" ,
bit operators, comparisons and
.BR && ,
.BR || .
Names, with or without
.BR $ ,
refer to environment variables (unset means 0).
Division truncates toward zero.
.SS Here-documents and here-strings
.B cmd <<WORD
reads lines up to a line consisting of
.B WORD
as the command's standard input.
Variables, command substitutions and arithmetic are expanded unless WORD is quoted (\fB<<'WORD'\fR).
.B <<\-WORD
strips leading tabs.
.B cmd <<< text
passes
.I text
and a newline.
At the prompt, continuation lines are read until the terminator.
.SH JOB CONTROL
In an interactive shell each job (a command or pipeline) runs in its own process group
and is given the terminal while it runs in the foreground.
.B Ctrl\-Z
stops it and returns to the prompt (status 148),
.B Ctrl\-C
interrupts only the foreground job.
.TP
.B jobs
List jobs.
.TP
.BR "fg " [ %n ]
Resume a job in the foreground (the most recent stopped job by default).
.TP
.BR "bg " [ %n ]
Resume a stopped job in the background.
.TP
.BR "kill " [ \-SIGNAL "] " %n | pid
Send a signal (default TERM) to a job or process;
.B kill \-l
lists signal names.
.TP
.B wait
Wait for background jobs.
.PP
Finished background jobs are reported before the next prompt.
.B exit
and
.B Ctrl\-D
warn once if there are stopped jobs; background jobs are sent SIGHUP when the shell exits.
Terminal modes are restored after every foreground job.
In scripts there is no terminal handling; background jobs get
.I /dev/null
as input and their own process group.
.SS Builtins
.BR cd ,
.BR pwd ,
.BR exit ,
.BR quit ,
.BR export ,
.BR unset ,
.BR alias ,
.BR unalias ,
.BR jobs ,
.BR source ,
.BR . ,
.BR history ,
.BR type ,
.BR fg ,
.BR bg ,
.BR wait ,
.BR kill ,
.BR help .
.SH LINE EDITING
.TP
.B Autosuggestion
The most recent history entry that starts with the current line is shown in grey.
.BR Right ,
.B End
or
.B Ctrl\-F
accept it;
.B Alt\-F
or
.B Ctrl\-Right
accept one word.
.TP
.B Highlighting
Known commands are blue, unknown commands red, quoted strings yellow, operators
cyan and existing paths underlined.
Python lines are highlighted as Python.
.TP
.BR Up / Down
Move through history entries that start with the text typed so far.
.TP
.B Ctrl\-R
Open the history search menu.
Typing filters the entries (case\-insensitive substring), Up/Down/Ctrl\-R/Tab
move the selection,
.B Enter
puts the entry on the command line without running it, and
.B Esc
cancels.
.TP
.BR Tab ", " Shift\-Tab
Complete.
A unique match is inserted; otherwise the common prefix is inserted and a menu with
descriptions is shown.
Further Tab presses cycle through it,
.B Enter
runs the line with the selection as it stands (selecting a command and pressing Enter runs
it with no arguments), and
.B Esc
cancels.
Commands are described from the man\-db index, files by type or size,
Python attributes by type, and options by parsing
.IB command " \-\-help"
(only when completing a word that begins with
.BR \- ).
.TP
.B Other keys
.B Ctrl\-A
.B Ctrl\-E
.B Ctrl\-B
.B Ctrl\-K
.B Ctrl\-U
.B Ctrl\-W
.B Ctrl\-L
.B Ctrl\-D
.B Alt\-B
.BR Alt\-D .
.SH CONFIGURATION
.I ~/.pyshrc.py
is ordinary Python run in the interactive namespace, so any of these can be set there:
.TP
.B aliases["name"] = "command"
Define aliases.
Interactive shells start with
.B ls
aliased to
.B "ls \-\-color=auto"
when the system
.B ls
accepts that option; replace it, or
.B del aliases["ls"]
to remove it.
.TP
.B prompt
A string, or a function returning one.
The default shows
.BR user@host ,
the working directory with parent names shortened, the git branch in white,
and the status of the last command when it failed.
.TP
.B complete_ignore
Tuple of file name suffixes never offered when completing command names
(default: a list of Windows executable suffixes such as
.BR .exe " and " .dll ).
.TP
.B complete_ignore_dirs
Tuple of
.B PATH
directories skipped when completing command names (default:
.BR /mnt/c ).
.PP
When a command's output does not end with a newline, the next prompt starts on a new row and a
reverse\-video
.B %
shows where the output stopped.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.pyshrc.py
Python executed in the interactive namespace at startup.
Define
.B prompt
(a string or a function returning one) to change the prompt.
.TP
.I ~/.pysh_history
Interactive history, one line per entry.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PYSH_HOME
Python home directory (the prefix containing
.IR lib/pythonX.Y ).
The default is the prefix of the Python used to build pysh.
.TP
.B PYSH_HISTFILE
History file.
.TP
.B PYSH_TTYLOG
Append everything the line editor writes to the terminal to this file (for bug reports about
screen redraws).
.TP
.B PYTHONHOME
Takes precedence over
.BR PYSH_HOME .
.SH EXIT STATUS
The exit status of a script or
.B \-c
command is that of the last command run.
Commands that cannot be found return 127.
.SH BUILDS
The default build links libpython into the executable and exports its symbols,
so every Python C extension module that the system
.B python
can load (ssl, sqlite3, lxml, numpy, ...) also loads in pysh.
It depends on the shared C library and a few others (libexpat, libz, libcrypto).
.PP
.B make static
builds
.BR pysh-static ,
a fully statically linked executable with no shared library dependencies.
It cannot load C extension modules (\fB.so\fR files).
Modules compiled into the interpreter, such as math, socket, zlib, json and
hashlib, are available; sqlite3, ssl, ctypes, bz2 and lzma are not.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Shell variables other than environment variables do not exist: use Python variables.
There are no subshells, functions, loops or conditionals in the shell syntax.
Job control needs Python 3.11 or later.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR python3 (1),
.BR bash (1),
.BR fish (1)
